WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.546
MCP systems move data through prompts, user files, retrieved resources, tool results,

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:08.252
server metadata, logs, cached content, memory features, and downstream services.

00:00:08.252 --> 00:00:09.734
Each movement changes exposure.

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:14.181
A file that was safe in a private workspace may become riskier

00:00:14.181 --> 00:00:16.404
if placed into a model context,

00:00:16.404 --> 00:00:21.221
copied into logs, cached for later use, or sent to a remote server.

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:24.926
Data handling rules should describe what can enter the system,

00:00:24.926 --> 00:00:29.002
where it can be stored, and how long it can remain.

00:00:29.002 --> 00:00:33.078
Trust boundaries are points where data, identity, permissions, or assumptions change.

00:00:33.078 --> 00:00:36.784
User input crosses a boundary when it influences model behavior.

00:00:36.784 --> 00:00:40.118
External content crosses a boundary when it enters context.

00:00:40.118 --> 00:00:46.047
Tool results cross a boundary when they move from a downstream system back to the host.

00:00:46.047 --> 00:00:50.123
Server metadata crosses a boundary when it influences model tool selection.

00:00:50.123 --> 00:00:54.569
Data flow diagrams should show these transitions instead of treating the AI

00:00:54.569 --> 00:00:56.422
application as one opaque box.

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:59.386
External content should be treated as untrusted input.

00:00:59.386 --> 00:01:04.574
A document, web page, issue, email, repository file, or resource may contain inaccurate, stale,

00:01:04.574 --> 00:01:05.686
or misleading text.

00:01:05.686 --> 00:01:07.909
That content can still be useful,

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:11.244
but it should not override trusted configuration or policy.

00:01:11.244 --> 00:01:15.320
Provenance helps the system and user understand where content came from.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:20.507
Tenant isolation and per-user filtering prevent one user's data from becoming another user's context.

00:01:20.507 --> 00:01:23.842
Data minimization reduces how much sensitive information is exposed.

00:01:23.842 --> 00:01:26.065
Retention and logging require careful design.

00:01:26.065 --> 00:01:30.141
Logs can support investigation, but they should avoid unnecessary secrets, credentials,

00:01:30.141 --> 00:01:32.365
regulated data, or sensitive user content.

00:01:32.365 --> 00:01:37.552
Cached content and memory can improve usability, but they need boundaries and deletion rules.

00:01:37.552 --> 00:01:41.999
Sensitive data should be protected before it reaches the model where possible.

00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:44.963
MCP fundamentals are not only about connecting systems;

00:01:44.963 --> 00:01:49.780
they are about keeping information understandable and controlled as it moves across systems.
